turnerjer0000@yahoo.com (Jeremy Turner) wrote in message news:<85891c40.0406251319.5bb8f8b7@posting.google.com>...
> "brutal", i like... i agree with [everyone who's said this] that there
> are too few big, unusual challenges (e.g. big room, zoos, graveyards,
> throne rooms, etc)

Big, unusual challenges are great, but monsters need not be the only
source.  That's one of the excu-- um, *reasons* I have for making the
terrain patch, to try to liven up ordinary dungeon levels in a dynamic
way.

> imo, slash'em takes the wrong aproach with its proliferation of
> special "lair" levels... i would prefer more unusual-but-not-unique
> features.  slash'em's "bathrooms" are a good example of what I think
> we should see more of.

I like the idea of lairs, but I think they're a little too common in
Slash'em.  One or two a game, selected randomly, seems to be about
right.

> forget who the OP was, but someone recently posted some excellent
> ideas for features like "underground river" levels with bridges (and
> of course trolls!)... "mini-castles" embedded in otherwise normal
> levels...

I may be who you're talking about, but also I may not be.  I haven't
yet decided if drawbridges over rivers should make it into the patch,
but the more I think about it the more I like it.  The challenge will
be in placing them where they make sense.

> gehennom in particular would benefit from the inclusion of more
> features like these... would help combat the tedium of "yet another
> maze level"...

The river code works on arbitrary level maps, so just applying the
function to a Genhennom maze, maybe with a flag passed for lava,
should work fine.  Lava's a lot more dangerous than water, however,
with a much greater chance for instadeath if the player presses one
key too many.  Also, I'd have to figure out how to add a new keyword
to the level compiler so special level builders could forbid lava
floes if they wanted.

> a few more yanis along the lines i'm talking about:
> 
> "nursery" rooms, featuring lots of hatchable eggs, baby dragons and
> nagas and gargoyles (oh my!), overseen by "matronly" adult dragons,
> nagas, gargoyles, and maybe a nurse... (new monster: naga nurse?)

I like this idea.  I think, ideally, there should be one (female)
monster, matching the eggs in the room (except, perhaps one egg in
twenty would actually be a cuckoo egg....).  Also, cockatrice nests
really need eggs.

I think the "zoo" types of special rooms are a bit overdone these
days.  The island room is my own small stab at remedying that.

> specific-monster-oriented "villages"... e.g. a small population of
> intrepid hobbits have established a settlement (surrounded by moat or
> undiggable walls) deep in the dungeons... perhaps a chance of a single
> shop or temple.  to retain balance, these settlements might have grown
> up around already-normally-generated shops or temples.  (i've always
> thought it strange that someone would build a shop deep in the
> dungeons where hardly anyone ever goes... this would give some
> rationale for that delicatessen on level 20)

Anything that adds rationale to the dungeon is an absolute good. 
Adding those little bits to add personality to the dungeon, that's
what makes Nethack, Nethack, to me.  Some of them could even add
challenge to the game (like maybe evading soldiers going out for
periodic dungeon patrolls?)

One of the things I hope to do with the terrain patch is add more
types of random levels.  I like the idea of randomly-generated towns
better than the current philosophy of making a large number of
specialized towns that get picked from each game.  Both have their
place, of course.  I just tend to give an edge to game elements that
can't be memorized.

> "tribal warfare" levels: essentially, a level with two of these
> "villages" vying for control of, say, a temple or group of
> fountains... which leads me to...

Check out the grudge patch, a lot of the coding behind this already
exists.  Just plop a squad of elves and a squad of orcs on a level and
watch the fun.

> "smithy": perhaps attached to armor or weapon shop... contains an
> anvil (new item), a forge (new dungeon feature), and of course a
> smith.  bring the smith a +0-or-less weapon or armor, and for a
> liberal amount of gold, he +1s it (but doesn't change buc status). 
> item must be made of metal.  if you want to spend a LOT of gold, you
> could fix those -3 gauntlets of power.

The problem behind this is that, eventually, the player gets strong
enough to kill money-accepting monsters and get his cash back. 
Blacksmiths sound as if they'd be useful even into the late game, so
the cost of the service wouldn't carry much weight.  This is because
the Nethack philosophy behind service providers is that they're
peaceful monsters.

Maybe they could be given the protection of a smith god?  And yet, I'd
hate to see it made *impossible* to kill smiths.

> "arena": a room with an "aura of conflict"... monsters duking it out
> for the amusement of some foo lords or foo kings.  this one could be
> highly abusable, but it just sounds so cool! ;)

Again, see the grudge patch.  This could be cool, actually.


